+// What is on the screen, kept in ordinary memory.
+//
+// A framebuffer is quick to write and desperately slow to read: a write is
+// posted and forgotten, a read is a round trip to the card that nothing can
+// buffer. The cross-fade on the way to an operating system begins by reading
+// back the band the menu lives in -- thirteen megabytes -- and that read is the
+// pause between pressing Enter and seeing anything happen.
+//
+// So nothing is ever read back. Every write to the screen is mirrored here, and
+// the mirror is what gets read. It costs one copy in ordinary memory, which at
+// 35 GB/s is beneath noticing, and it is exact: it holds what was drawn, which
+// is what the reader wanted to know.
+//
+// It is only trusted once a whole screen has gone through it, and it is dropped
+// whenever something else might have drawn -- text mode, or a program rEFInd
+// launched and came back from.
+static EG_IMAGE *Shadow      = NULL;
